{
  "id": "Compound::DB08910 - Gene::1576",
  "verbalization": "Pomalidomide is implicated in the activation of Cyp3a4 through a series of interconnected pathways involving antineoplastic agents and organic compounds. The evidence suggests that Pomalidomide, as a cause of neutropenic sepsis, pneumocystis pneumonia, and venous thromboembolism, leads to the binding of Cyp3a4 through the action of Trabectedin, Temozolomide, and Fenofibrate, respectively. These agents, all classified as antineoplastic agents, are linked to Cyp3a4 via their molecular interactions, highlighting a recurring mechanism of enzyme induction. The shared lowest common ancestors—Organonitrogen Heterocyclic Compound, Organic Heterobicyclic Compound, and Organic Aromatic Compound—suggest a common pharmacological profile among the implicated agents, reinforcing the role of Cyp3a4 as a target in these pathways. The integration of these findings implies that Pomalidomide may indirectly influence Cyp3a4 activity through the metabolic activation of these antineoplastic compounds, underscoring a potential role in drug metabolism and toxicity.",
